<?php require __DIR__.'/../includes/bootstrap.php';$post=post_by_slug('search-console-multimodal-search-seo-measure') ?? ['slug'=>'search-console-multimodal-search-seo-measure','title'=>'Search Console Multimodal Search: What SEOs Should Measure','description'=>'Google added multimodal Search reporting to Search Console. Learn what the new filter measures, how to analyze it, and what SEOs should track next today.','category'=>'Tools Guide','date'=>'2026-09-25','read_time'=>'9 min read'];ob_start(); ?><p>Google Search Console added reporting for web multimodal Search on September 24, 2026. Google says the new filter is available in Search performance reports and in the Generative AI features report. It covers visual-search experiences including Lens, Circle to Search on Android, image uploads to Google Search and Chrome's right-click “Search this image” feature.</p><figure class="article-image"><img src="/assets/images/blog/search-console-multimodal-search-seo-measure.svg" width="1200" height="630" alt="Google Search Console multimodal Search reporting for SEO measurement"></figure><p>The rollout is global and gives publishers a way to measure how their content is surfaced when people start searches with visual inputs. For SEO teams, the main change is measurement: this demand can now be separated from broader web-search numbers.</p><h2>What the new filter measures</h2><p>Google says the multimodal search type filter can be used in the Performance report. The data covers web-search interactions where the user supplies an image or uses a visual search feature. The amount of data shown depends on whether your site receives traffic from those experiences.</p><p>Google also says the reporting is available in the Generative AI features report. Keep the dimensions separate because multimodal input and generative search are not the same thing.</p><h2>Why multimodal search matters</h2><p>Text search starts with words. Multimodal search can start with an object, screenshot, photograph or visual context. A product page may be discovered from an image before the user knows the product name. A tutorial may be surfaced after someone photographs a device or screen.</p><p>This does not mean every page needs a visual redesign. It means teams should understand whether images and visual explanations are part of the search journey for their topic.</p><h2>What to measure</h2><table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th>Metric</th><th>Question</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Impressions</td><td>Is multimodal demand increasing?</td></tr><tr><td>Clicks</td><td>Which pages turn visual discovery into visits?</td></tr><tr><td>CTR</td><td>Does the page match the search intent?</td></tr><tr><td>Average position</td><td>How visible are the pages?</td></tr><tr><td>Landing pages</td><td>Which content types benefit?</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Do not compare one new day's data with a mature web-search series. Establish a baseline over several weeks. The rollout is new and the amount of available data can vary by site.</p><h2>Build a multimodal SEO segment</h2><p>Create a dedicated dashboard segment for multimodal impressions, clicks, CTR and top landing pages. Group pages into products, services, tutorials, locations and editorial content. Then compare those groups with image relevance, alt text, surrounding explanation, dimensions, mobile rendering and page speed.</p><h2>Five checks for pages receiving impressions</h2><ol><li>Does the image clearly represent the page subject?</li><li>Is the alt text descriptive and useful?</li><li>Does nearby text explain the visual?</li><li>Can the visitor complete the task after arriving?</li><li>Does the image load and scale reliably on mobile?</li></ol><h2>Do not confuse multimodal Search with image search</h2><p>Google's announcement is specifically about web multimodal Search reporting. It is broader than treating the report as a simple image-ranking report. Users can start with Lens, Circle to Search, an uploaded image or a browser image-search action and then receive web results.</p><p>Use Google's documented filter rather than trying to infer the source from referral strings alone.</p><h2>How this changes content planning</h2><p>Once enough data accumulates, compare pages receiving multimodal impressions with pages that do not. Look for patterns in useful content types. These are hypotheses to test, not rules to assume.</p><p>The feedback loop is simple: Search Console shows what visual searchers are finding, while a content audit explains what those pages do well. Improve pages based on evidence rather than publishing more images simply because visual search is growing.</p><p>See <a href="/tools-guide/search-console-url-inspection-audit-workflow">Search Console URL Inspection: A Better SEO Audit Workflow</a>.</p><h2>Practical takeaways</h2><ul><li>Use the new filter as a separate reporting segment.</li><li>Track landing pages, impressions, clicks and CTR before changing content.</li><li>Audit images for relevance, accessibility, context and delivery.</li><li>Use several weeks of data before large decisions.</li><li>Do not treat multimodal visibility as identical to traditional image-search performance.</li></ul><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>When did Search Console add multimodal reporting?</h3><p>Google announced it on September 24, 2026 and said the rollout was starting globally that day.</p><h3>Which experiences are included?</h3><p>Google lists Lens, Circle to Search on Android, image uploads to Google Search and Chrome's right-click “Search this image”.</p><h3>Where can I find the data?</h3><p>Google says the filter is available in Search performance reports and the Generative AI features report.</p><h3>Should I change every page now?</h3><p>No. First see whether your site receives multimodal impressions and inspect the pages associated with that traffic.</p><h2>Sources</h2><ul><li><a href="https://developers.google.com/search/blog/2026/09/web-multimodal-in-sc">Google Search Central — Multimodal Search performance reporting</a></li><li><a href="https://support.google.com/webmasters/answer/7042828">Google Search Console Help — Performance report</a></li></ul><?php $articleHtml=ob_get_clean();require __DIR__.'/../includes/blog-template.php';